'use strict';
const {course,section:s,worked:w}=require('./web-helper');
const units=[
{id:'web-request-render',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Trace a page from navigation to pixels',introduction:'A student opens a timetable URL. Several systems cooperate before a useful page appears: locating the server, exchanging a response, parsing documents and drawing the result. Separating these stages makes a blank page a diagnosable event rather than a single mysterious failure.',sections:[s('A request is a message, not a screen','The browser is the user agent: it requests a resource identified by a URL. A server receives an HTTP request and returns a status, response headers and usually a body. The body might be HTML, an image, JSON or an error representation. A successful connection is not proof that the requested page exists.','HTML describes document structure. As the browser parses it, linked stylesheets, scripts, fonts and images may cause further requests. The address-bar navigation and each subresource request have their own result; one successful HTML response does not guarantee every dependency loaded.'),s('Rendering is staged and observable','The browser builds a document object model from HTML and style information from CSS. It combines applicable information, calculates layout and paints pixels; later changes can trigger further layout or painting. JavaScript may modify the document or request more data, so page appearance can change after the initial response.','Do not infer that the server rendered a button simply because a button is visible. Server code, document markup, style rules and browser scripts have different responsibilities. The bounded trace below models dependencies only: it neither implements a rendering engine nor predicts exact browser scheduling.')],worked:w('events = ["navigate", "request HTML", "parse HTML", "request CSS", "layout", "paint"]\nprint(events.index("parse HTML") < events.index("layout"))\nprint(len([x for x in events if x.startswith("request")]))','True\n2','Check the declared ordering and number of resource requests in a synthetic page trace.',['Read the fixed event sequence.','Locate parsing and layout positions.','Count distinct request events.'],'The assertions concern the supplied trace. Real browsers may overlap network, parsing and script execution; exact timing requires browser evidence.'),mistakes:['Treating one HTML request as every request made by a page.','Calling a visible UI state proof that every API request succeeded.'],takeaways:['Distinguish HTTP responses from browser rendering.','Trace dependencies and stage failures before proposing a fix.'],quiz:[
['What does the server return to an HTTP request?','A response message','A guaranteed painted screen','The browser’s local storage','The final assistive-technology tree','HTTP supplies status, headers and a representation; rendering happens in the user agent.','Painting is browser work.','Local storage is client state.','Accessibility representation is computed in the browser.'],
['Which resource can trigger additional page requests?','An HTML document','Only the operating-system clock','Only a local screenshot','Only a bookmark title','HTML may reference stylesheets, scripts, images and other resources that need retrieval.','The clock does not describe document dependencies.','A screenshot does not request linked resources.','A bookmark title is not a dependency graph.'],
['What does layout determine in a simplified rendering pipeline?','Element geometry','The authoritative account role','The DNS record owner','The validity of a server certificate','Layout computes positions and sizes from document and style information.','Roles need trusted application policy.','DNS ownership is separate.','Certificate verification is a transport concern.'],
['A timetable HTML request succeeds but its stylesheet fails. What should a reviewer expect?','Content may appear unstyled','The HTML must have failed too','The DNS cache must be empty','The database is necessarily deleted','Subresources have independent outcomes, so successful HTML can appear without its intended styling.','Independent requests can have different results.','The trace does not establish a DNS-cache cause.','A stylesheet failure does not establish data deletion.'],
['A browser shows a spinner after the document loads. Which evidence narrows the issue?','The pending data request','Only the page filename','Only the logo’s dimensions','Only the title’s spelling','A separately pending API request can explain a loading state after HTML has arrived.','A filename does not expose the request lifecycle.','Logo dimensions do not show data completion.','Title spelling does not settle request state.'],
['A support agent calls the page fully loaded because status 200 appeared once. What is missing?','Other dependency outcomes','A new database password','A second domain purchase','A longer account display name','One successful response does not prove all dependencies or application behavior succeeded.','Changing credentials is not justified by this evidence.','A new domain does not inspect dependencies.','Display-name length is unrelated.'],
['A script changes the DOM after the first paint. Which statement is sound?','Later rendering work may occur','The first HTML response is rewritten in transit','DNS must run once per DOM node','The server always executes the script','DOM changes may require style, layout or paint work after initial display.','A local change does not rewrite a delivered response.','DNS resolves hosts, not nodes.','Browser scripts can execute locally.'],
['A team needs exact request timing rather than a conceptual trace. What should it inspect?','A recorded browser waterfall','Only this model’s list order','Only a prose diagram','Only the route’s marketing name','Measured network/browser evidence is needed for actual scheduling and duration claims.','A fixed list declares rather than measures order.','A diagram is not timing evidence.','A marketing name contains no timing data.']
]},
{id:'web-url-dns',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Read URLs and separate DNS from hosting',introduction:'A logistics team moves its customer portal to a new host without changing the public name. Understanding the URL and DNS boundary helps distinguish a routing mistake from an application route that does not exist.',sections:[s('Identify the resource address precisely','A URL includes a scheme, an authority such as a host and optional port, a path and possibly a query and fragment. In https://portal.example.test/orders?state=open#summary, the path and query select a server resource representation, while the fragment normally identifies a location interpreted by the client. Do not place credentials or private student details in URLs because histories and logs may retain them.','A domain is a human-usable name; hosting supplies the service that answers requests. DNS maps names to records such as address records, with caching according to record lifetimes. Registering a domain alone does not create an application or deploy its files.'),s('Follow a change across independent boundaries','A name can resolve to an address while the web service on that host is unavailable. Conversely, a working server can be unreachable through a misspelled domain or stale record. HTTPS also needs the intended hostname to match the server’s certificate and service configuration.','The Python parser below checks address components, not DNS resolution, certificate trust or server availability. A deployment review should record the intended host, route and change window, and use bounded read-only checks rather than guessing from one error message.')],worked:w('from urllib.parse import urlsplit, parse_qs\nu = urlsplit("https://portal.example.test/orders?state=open#summary")\nprint(u.hostname)\nprint(u.path, parse_qs(u.query)["state"][0])\nprint(u.fragment)','portal.example.test\n/orders open\nsummary','Separate a synthetic portal URL into host, resource selection and client fragment.',['Parse the supplied absolute URL.','Read host, path and query independently.','Inspect the fragment without making a request.'],'Parsing confirms the string’s components only. It does not prove a domain resolves or that the resource exists.'),mistakes:['Buying a domain and assuming files are automatically hosted.','Treating a URL fragment as a server authorization boundary.'],takeaways:['Read scheme, host, path, query and fragment separately.','Check name resolution, service reachability and application routing as different stages.'],quiz:[
['Which URL component identifies the host name?','The authority’s host','The fragment text','The last query value','The page heading','The host belongs to the authority; paths, queries and fragments serve different addressing purposes.','A fragment is normally client interpreted.','Query values are resource input.','A heading is document content.'],
['What does hosting provide?','A running service or files','Ownership of every domain','A replacement for all DNS records','Automatic semantic HTML','Hosting supplies infrastructure or a service capable of answering requests.','Domain registration is separate.','DNS still maps the chosen name.','Hosting does not author content semantics.'],
['Which part is normally not sent as the HTTP target?','The URL fragment','The path','The query string','The host information','The fragment is handled by the user agent rather than sent as the request target.','The path identifies the resource target.','Queries can be part of the target.','Host information is needed for authority/routing.'],
['A portal name resolves but the service port is closed. What follows?','DNS success is insufficient','The HTML is certainly invalid','The browser must ignore HTTPS','The record cache proves application health','Name resolution and service availability are independent boundaries.','No document was inspected.','Disabling HTTPS is not a repair.','A cached name is not a health result.'],
['A team changes the hosting provider but keeps the public domain. What needs review?','DNS and host configuration','Only the page font','Only the logo filename','Only the user’s timezone','The name must route to the new intended service, which must accept that hostname.','Fonts do not direct name resolution.','Logo names do not configure hosts.','Timezone does not route the domain.'],
['A URL contains ?student_email=private. Why is that risky?','URLs may be retained in logs','Queries are always encrypted at rest','Fragments are automatically anonymized','DNS removes personal information','URL strings can persist in browser history, logs and operational tooling.','Transport does not ensure storage privacy.','Fragments are not automatic anonymization.','DNS does not sanitize application inputs.'],
['A path returns 404 on an otherwise reachable host. Which boundary is most directly implicated?','The resource route','The domain purchase date','The CSS box model','The local screen resolution','A reachable service can report that a specific requested resource is not found.','Registration age does not identify a missing route.','Layout is not server routing.','Screen size does not define resource existence.'],
['A parser accepts a URL string. What claim remains unverified?','The site is reachable','The scheme was extracted','The path was separated','The fragment was located','Syntactic parsing does not contact DNS or an HTTP service and cannot establish reachability.','These are parser outputs in the fixture.','The fixture explicitly extracts the path.','The fixture explicitly identifies the fragment.']
]},
{id:'web-response-diagnosis',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Distinguish transport, HTTP and application failures',introduction:'A support dashboard may show an error because a connection failed, a resource was denied, or a successful JSON response contained no records. Reporting the layer and observed result makes engineering handoffs much more useful.',sections:[s('Classify before changing configuration','A network failure can occur before an HTTP response exists. If a response arrives, its status class communicates a protocol result: 2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx a client-related request problem and 5xx server failure. Interpret the specific status and the application contract rather than calling every problem a browser bug.','A 200 response can carry an empty list, and an application can render an error after parsing a successful response incorrectly. A 404 does not establish that the entire server is offline. Distinguish status, representation body, parsing and UI state in a report.'),s('Preserve evidence and avoid unsafe fixes','Record the intended URL with sensitive fields removed, the action, approximate time, response status or connection error, and a reproducible synthetic case. DevTools can help identify the failed resource, initiator and response body under the account’s authorized access.','Do not share raw cookies, bearer values or private records in screenshots or copied network exports. Do not disable certificate checks, remove authorization or publish debug secrets because a page is failing. The fixture is a simple classification function for supplied observations, not a network probe.')],worked:w('def classify(status):\n    if status is None: return "no HTTP response"\n    if status >= 500: return "server failure"\n    if status >= 400: return "request failure"\n    return "inspect successful response contract"\nfor status in [None, 404, 503, 200]:\n    print(classify(status))','no HTTP response\nrequest failure\nserver failure\ninspect successful response contract','Classify synthetic observations without contacting a live service.',['Separate absence of HTTP from received status.','Check server and request failure classes.','Keep successful representation interpretation distinct.'],'The categories are introductory. Actual redirect handling, authentication and service contracts require the specific response details.'),mistakes:['Equating every error view with an HTTP 500.','Sharing copied request headers without removing credentials.'],takeaways:['Describe the failed layer and actual evidence.','A successful HTTP status does not prove correct application interpretation.'],quiz:[
['A connection fails before any response arrives. What should the report say?','No HTTP response observed','HTTP 500 was confirmed','The HTML must be invalid','The server returned an empty array','Without a received response, assigning an HTTP status invents evidence.','A 500 requires an observed response.','No HTML was inspected.','An array requires response content.'],
['What does HTTP status 404 most directly describe?','The target was not found','Every service is offline','The browser has no memory','The domain is unregistered','A 404 is a response about the requested resource, not proof that all infrastructure failed.','A service supplied a response.','Memory state is not conveyed by this status.','Registration is not established by a resource status.'],
['What should accompany a useful bug report?','Reproducible observed evidence','The user’s raw session cookie','A complete private grade export','An invented server cause','Useful reports state actions and actual results while protecting sensitive data.','Session cookies are credentials.','Private records should be minimized.','A cause must be supported by evidence.'],
['An API returns 200 with [] and the page shows no orders. What should be checked?','The empty-result contract','Only certificate expiry','Only domain registration','Only the monitor brightness','A successful empty representation may be valid or mishandled according to the application contract.','The response already arrived over its connection.','Registration does not decide empty-result rendering.','Brightness does not inspect the representation.'],
['A copied HAR file includes authorization values. What should happen before sharing?','Remove sensitive content','Publish it to every student','Rename it as a screenshot','Assume HTTPS erased the keys','Network exports can contain reusable credentials and private data even when transport used HTTPS.','Wider sharing expands exposure.','Renaming does not remove contents.','TLS does not sanitize saved exports.'],
['A resource shows 503. Which first statement is justified?','The server reported unavailable','A specific database table was deleted','The student mistyped a password','DNS could not resolve the hostname','The status identifies a service-level unavailability response but not its underlying cause.','Deletion is an unsupported diagnosis.','Password behavior needs evidence.','A response arrived, so this request passed name resolution.'],
['A developer fixes a certificate error by disabling verification. What is the concern?','The trust check is bypassed','The route becomes semantic HTML','Cookies become harmless text','The server is automatically authenticated','Skipping verification removes an essential peer/hostname trust boundary.','HTML semantics are unrelated.','Cookies can remain credentials.','Authentication assurance is weakened rather than established.'],
['A page parses JSON as HTML and fails after status 200. Where is the likely mismatch?','Content interpretation','The numeric status class','The domain renewal policy','The user’s monitor cable','Client expectations must match the delivered media type and response contract.','A successful status alone is not the parser contract.','Renewal policy does not choose a parser.','Display hardware does not parse JSON.']
]},
{id:'web-inspect-elements',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Inspect document structure and computed styles',introduction:'An administrator reports that an enrollment button exists but cannot be seen. DevTools lets a developer inspect the current element, its applied rules and geometry instead of guessing from the source filename.',sections:[s('Inspect the current document, not just saved source','The Elements panel shows the browser’s current DOM, including changes made by scripts. View Source is closer to the delivered HTML document; the two can differ. Select the actual element and inspect its attributes, ancestor structure and applicable style rules before changing application code.','Computed styles show resolved values after the cascade. A crossed-out declaration was not the winning declaration for that property. Hidden ancestors, display, visibility, dimensions and overlapping content can all affect what a person sees; a matching selector alone does not establish visibility.'),s('Use local edits as experiments','A DevTools style edit can test a hypothesis temporarily in your browser. It does not update the repository or production deployment. Once the cause is understood, make the smallest appropriate source change, review its effects and test normal responsive and keyboard behavior.','The fixture reads a synthetic style record and reports a simple hidden-state rule. A real browser has a much richer cascade, layout and accessibility model. The exercise teaches how to state the assumption being tested, not how to replace computed-style inspection.')],worked:w('element = {"tag": "button", "text": "Enroll", "computed": {"display": "none"}}\nprint(element["tag"])\nprint(element["computed"]["display"] == "none")\nelement["computed"]["display"] = "inline-block"\nprint(element["computed"]["display"])','button\nTrue\ninline-block','Inspect and temporarily change a supplied style observation.',['Read the selected element’s type.','Inspect the declared computed display.','Change only the local fixture value.'],'The fixture mutation is analogous to a local experiment, not a persistent source or deployment change.'),mistakes:['Assuming editing a DevTools rule saves the repository.','Reading only an element’s own rule while ignoring hidden ancestors.'],takeaways:['Inspect the live DOM and winning style values.','Convert a successful temporary experiment into a reviewed source change.'],quiz:[
['What does the Elements panel primarily inspect?','The current DOM','Only the Git commit history','Only DNS records','The private database schema','Elements exposes the browser’s document representation and related styles.','Git history is a separate tool.','DNS is network naming.','Database internals are not the DOM.'],
['What does a crossed-out CSS declaration usually indicate?','It did not win that property','It was deleted from Git','The server rejected the HTML','The element has no parent','DevTools marks declarations overridden or otherwise not applied under the cascade.','Local style display does not delete commits.','It is not an HTTP rejection message.','An overridden declaration says nothing about parent existence.'],
['Does a temporary DevTools edit deploy a fix?','No, it changes local state','Yes, every visitor gets it','Yes, Git commits automatically','Yes, server files are overwritten','Browser experiments normally affect only the local page session.','Other users are not updated.','Git is not automatically committed.','Server files are not written by a local style edit.'],
['A button’s own display is visible but its ancestor is hidden. What should be inspected?','The ancestor chain','Only the button text','Only the server hostname','Only the file extension','Ancestor styling can prevent descendant rendering despite the child’s own declaration.','Text alone does not reveal ancestor styles.','Hostnames do not explain this observed style state.','Extensions do not determine computed ancestor visibility.'],
['A script appends an error panel after load. Where is it most directly inspected?','The live DOM panel','Only original View Source','Only the package lockfile','Only the DNS console','The current DOM includes runtime modifications that may not be present in the original HTML.','Original source need not contain runtime nodes.','Dependencies do not display current nodes.','DNS tools do not display document changes.'],
['A local rule change fixes overlap at one width. What is the next engineering step?','Review and test a source fix','Tell all users to edit DevTools','Delete all responsive rules','Disable browser zoom','A controlled source change needs responsive and accessibility validation before release.','User experiments are not a maintainable deployment.','Deleting rules may create regressions.','Zoom is an accessibility capability, not the cause by default.'],
['A CSS selector matches, but the control remains offscreen. Which evidence matters?','Layout context','Only selector spelling','Only the page title','Only response status 200','Matching is only one stage; geometry and surrounding layout affect visible placement.','A correctly spelled selector is not a layout proof.','Titles do not determine element placement.','HTTP success does not guarantee useful rendering.'],
['What limitation applies to the Python style-record fixture?','It is not a CSS engine','It tests every cascade origin','It computes real browser layout','It verifies screen-reader output','The dictionary models declared observations without parsing CSS or running a browser.','Cascade origins are not implemented.','There is no layout calculation.','Accessibility-tree behavior is not executed.']
]},
{id:'web-network-console',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Use network and console evidence responsibly',introduction:'A customer support page loads its shell but fails to display tickets. Network and Console panels answer different questions: which request failed, and which script error prevented the page from using its result?',sections:[s('Read request records as a timeline','The Network panel records requests while capture is active. Inspect the resource URL, method, status, initiator, timing and response. Preserve-log and disable-cache options change the experiment; record when you use them so another developer can reproduce the conditions. Filter by resource type, but remember that hiding rows does not mean those requests did not occur.','A request may be served from cache or intercepted by a service worker. A missing row can mean capture started too late, filtering hid it or no request occurred. Treat the panel as evidence gathered under stated conditions, not as an automatic complete history of all browsing.'),s('Use console messages as clues, not permissions','The Console reports errors and logged information from the current execution environment. A stack trace can identify code locations, but the visible line may be a symptom rather than the original failure. Check the selected frame/context and correlate a script error with the actual request result.','Do not paste unfamiliar code into the console merely because a webpage or stranger instructs you to. Console execution can act with the logged-in page’s authority. Share sanitized messages and a minimal synthetic reproduction instead of copied token values or full customer payloads.')],worked:w('requests = [{"url":"/tickets","status":200},{"url":"/tickets/summary","status":500},{"url":"/logo.svg","status":200}]\nfailed = [r["url"] for r in requests if r["status"] >= 400]\nprint(failed)\nprint("correlate with console; do not infer cause")','[\'/tickets/summary\']\ncorrelate with console; do not infer cause','Find failed resources in a sanitized synthetic capture.',['Review each supplied status.','Filter request failures without losing URL identity.','Keep the root-cause claim separate from the observation.'],'The list is pre-recorded test data. It does not collect a real browser trace or establish why the selected request failed.'),mistakes:['Opening DevTools after a request and assuming the empty panel proves no request occurred.','Pasting untrusted console instructions into an authenticated page.'],takeaways:['Correlate request and script evidence under reproducible conditions.','Protect page authority and private payloads during debugging.'],quiz:[
['Which panel most directly shows request status and timing?','Network','Only Elements','Only bookmarks','Only browser history','Network records individual resource transactions under the active capture conditions.','Elements focuses on the document and styles.','Bookmarks are saved addresses.','History is not a full per-request timing capture.'],
['What does a console stack trace provide?','Code-location clues','Automatic proof of root cause','A deployment approval','A replacement for server authorization','Stack traces help locate execution but require context to identify the underlying cause.','A symptom location may differ from the root cause.','Debug information is not an approval.','Client diagnostics do not enforce server permissions.'],
['Why avoid pasting unknown console code?','It can use page authority','It only changes font colour','It is always read-only','It cannot access any page data','Console code may execute with privileges available to scripts in that page context.','Its effects are not limited to presentation.','Execution can mutate state.','Page data may be accessible under browser rules.'],
['A request happened before Network capture began. What can an empty panel prove?','No captured row is present','No request ever occurred','The server is offline','The user never navigated','An observation window limits what the recording establishes.','Earlier events may not be retained.','An empty capture is not a reachability test.','History outside the window is unknown.'],
['A filtered capture displays only XHR entries. What about image requests?','Hidden by the filter','They are necessarily absent','They bypass HTTP entirely','They are always errors','Filtering changes visible rows without proving other resource types did not load.','Hidden is not absent.','Images can be HTTP resources.','Resource type does not imply failure.'],
['A developer disables cache for reproduction. What should the report include?','That experiment condition','Only a screenshot filename','A private session credential','A claim that every user has no cache','Cache settings can change behavior and must be documented for reproducibility.','A filename does not describe the condition.','Credentials should not be shared.','One local setting does not describe all users.'],
['The request succeeds but a TypeError follows while reading data. What needs correlation?','Payload/code contract','Only DNS TTL','Only certificate issuer','Only keyboard layout','A successful response can still mismatch the client’s expected data structure.','DNS does not define JSON property access.','Issuer identity does not define the data schema.','Keyboard layout does not explain this trace.'],
['A network response contains real patient details. What is appropriate debugging evidence?','A minimized synthetic reproduction','The entire public response export','A pasted raw cookie and payload','A screenshot sent without review','Data minimization protects private content while retaining the behavior needed to diagnose the issue.','Public sharing exposes sensitive records.','Raw credentials and records should be excluded.','Screenshots can retain private data too.']
]},
{id:'web-languages',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Assign HTML, CSS and JavaScript responsibilities',introduction:'A retail team builds a product card with a title, a price and an add-to-cart control. Choosing the right layer improves resilience: meaningful content should not disappear merely because a decorative effect or optional script fails.',sections:[s('Start with content and native behavior','HTML expresses structure and meaning: headings, paragraphs, links, lists and controls. A link navigates to a resource; a button performs an action. CSS defines presentation such as typography, colour, spacing and layout. JavaScript adds behavior, manages dynamic state and coordinates events or requests.','These are responsibilities rather than separate servers. A server can generate HTML and a script can change the DOM; neither fact erases the semantic purpose of the resulting elements. Prefer native controls and progressive enhancement so important tasks have a usable baseline where practical.'),s('Test the boundaries between layers','A cart button needs an accessible name and a clear busy/error state, not only a colour transition. A form can use native labels and constraints even if JavaScript adds better feedback. Client validation improves interaction, while the server still validates the actual operation and account authority.','The classification fixture represents design decisions as data. It cannot check a real accessibility tree, calculate styles or run browser JavaScript. Use it to explain responsibility, then validate actual rendered pages with keyboard, responsive and functional tests.')],worked:w('responsibility = {"product heading":"HTML", "card spacing":"CSS", "cart feedback":"JavaScript"}\nfor task in ["product heading","card spacing","cart feedback"]:\n    print(task + ": " + responsibility[task])','product heading: HTML\ncard spacing: CSS\ncart feedback: JavaScript','Classify three product-card responsibilities.',['Identify content meaning.','Separate presentation from dynamic feedback.','Review the explicit mapping rather than infer behavior from filenames.'],'The mapping illustrates a design division. It is not a parser or a test of the finished interface.'),mistakes:['Building a clickable div when a native button is appropriate.','Treating client validation as authoritative server enforcement.'],takeaways:['Use HTML meaning, CSS presentation and JavaScript behavior deliberately.','Preserve useful baseline interaction and test the integrated result.'],quiz:[
['Which layer gives a heading its structural meaning?','HTML','Only CSS colour','Only JavaScript timing','Only server uptime','A semantic heading element expresses document structure independently of its visual styling.','Colour does not create heading semantics.','Scheduling does not assign structure.','Uptime does not author document meaning.'],
['Which layer normally controls card spacing?','CSS','DNS','The database index','The cookie signature','CSS governs presentation and layout, including spacing.','DNS maps names.','Indexes optimize data access.','Signatures concern credential integrity.'],
['What is a native button suited to?','An interactive action','A document title only','An invisible DNS record','A replacement for all links','Buttons provide built-in interaction semantics for actions.','Titles are document metadata.','DNS is unrelated to document controls.','Navigation links remain useful.'],
['A cart interaction needs a loading state. Which layer can manage that dynamic state?','JavaScript behavior','Only a static colour token','Only a domain record','Only a file permission','Scripts can coordinate event-driven UI state while HTML/CSS provide meaningful structure and styling.','A colour token does not coordinate requests.','DNS does not track interaction state.','File permissions do not update the UI.'],
['A visual red outline is the only error indicator. What is missing?','A clear error message','A longer class name','A second domain','A hidden account role','Colour alone can be insufficient; users need perceivable, meaningful feedback.','Naming does not communicate the error to users.','Domains do not explain validation.','Hidden roles do not provide feedback.'],
['A browser form blocks an invalid age. Must the server still validate it?','Yes, validate the actual request','No, HTML is authoritative','No, CSS prevents direct requests','Only when JavaScript is absent','Client constraints improve usability but do not replace server-side request validation.','Browser checks can be bypassed.','Styling does not protect the server.','Requests need validation regardless of script availability.'],
['A team removes meaningful content when animation fails. What principle can improve resilience?','Progressive enhancement','Making all text a background image','Hiding every control by default','Relying on one browser extension','A usable baseline can survive optional presentation or behavior failures.','Images can lose meaning and adaptability.','Hidden controls remove baseline functionality.','Extensions are not a universal user dependency.'],
['A server generates a semantic navigation list. Which conclusion follows?','It is still HTML structure','Server output cannot be semantic','CSS becomes a database language','JavaScript must author every list','The authoring location does not change the meaning of the resulting HTML elements.','Generated markup can retain semantics.','CSS remains a presentation language.','Static or server-rendered lists are valid.']
]},
{id:'web-https-cookies',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Understand HTTPS and cookie boundaries as a user',introduction:'A student portal uses HTTPS and a sign-in cookie. Both mechanisms matter, but neither means every page is trustworthy or every account action is permitted. A professional should recognize the protection each supplies and what remains separate.',sections:[s('Transport trust has a defined purpose','HTTPS uses HTTP over TLS, protecting confidentiality and integrity in transit and authenticating the intended service through certificate validation. It does not prove that a business is honest, that content is accurate or that an application has no security bugs. Check the intended hostname, not just a padlock.','Cookies are values that browsers store and attach to matching requests according to scope and attributes. Some hold preferences; some identify an authenticated session. Secure restricts sending to secure contexts under browser rules, HttpOnly restricts script access, and SameSite affects cross-site sending. These attributes protect different aspects of handling.'),s('Keep credentials and permissions separate','Signing out should end the intended session according to the application’s policy; deleting visible page content is not necessarily server revocation. A student must not gain another person’s history by changing an id in a URL. The server must authorize the verified account for the requested object.','The fixture evaluates declared cookie flags for a proposed session configuration. It does not create browser cookies or test an actual server. Never log raw session values, paste them into support chats or disable TLS verification to suppress a warning.')],worked:w('cookie = {"secure": True, "httponly": True, "samesite": "Lax"}\nprint(cookie["secure"] and cookie["httponly"])\nprint(cookie["samesite"])\nprint("authorization still required")','True\nLax\nauthorization still required','Review a synthetic session-cookie policy without exposing a credential.',['Inspect transport and script-access flags.','Read the declared cross-site policy.','State the independent server authorization requirement.'],'These booleans describe fixture configuration only; they do not establish browser enforcement, revocation or every CSRF defense.'),mistakes:['Treating a padlock as proof of honest content.','Sharing session cookies as harmless debugging text.'],takeaways:['HTTPS protects a transport boundary, not all application behavior.','Treat session values as credentials and permissions as server decisions.'],quiz:[
['What does HTTPS primarily protect?','Data in transit','Every business claim','All database backups','Every server authorization rule','TLS protects the connection’s confidentiality/integrity and peer identity under its trust policy.','Transport cannot certify business honesty.','Backups have separate storage controls.','Application permissions need their own enforcement.'],
['Which cookie attribute limits script access?','HttpOnly','A longer cookie name','Path as encryption','Domain as a password hash','HttpOnly restricts access through browser script APIs under browser rules.','Name length does not impose this control.','Path is scope, not encryption.','Domain matching is not password hashing.'],
['Can a cookie be a credential?','Yes, a session value can be','No, cookies are always public','Only if its name says password','Only on unencrypted pages','Possession of some session values can represent account authority and requires protection.','Preference cookies exist, but not every cookie is harmless.','Authority depends on use, not naming.','HTTPS does not remove credential significance.'],
['A site has a valid certificate but deceptive content. What does the certificate establish?','Connection identity','The business is honest','Every article is correct','The refund policy is fair','Certificate validation authenticates the intended service connection, not the quality of its business claims.','Honesty is not a certificate property.','Content accuracy needs other evidence.','Policy fairness is not established by TLS.'],
['A student changes a URL id to another account’s history. What must the server check?','Object-level permission','Only the page colour','Only cookie string length','Only whether HTTPS is used','Verified identity must be authorized for the specific requested object.','Presentation does not enforce access.','Length does not establish permission.','TLS does not decide ownership.'],
['A support agent asks for the raw session cookie. What should the user do?','Use a safe support process instead','Post it in the public forum','Assume HttpOnly prevents sharing risk','Rename it before posting','Raw session values may let another holder act with account authority.','Public disclosure expands credential exposure.','HttpOnly does not protect a value deliberately copied elsewhere.','Renaming does not remove authority.'],
['A page disappears after clicking logout. What remains to verify?','Server session invalidation','Only that the background is white','Only the page title changed','Only browser zoom reset','A UI transition alone does not establish the credential lifecycle on the server.','Colour is not revocation evidence.','Titles do not invalidate credentials.','Zoom does not affect server authority.'],
['The cookie policy fixture prints True. What is still outside its evidence?','Deployed behavior','The supplied secure flag','The supplied HttpOnly flag','The literal SameSite value','A dictionary check does not exercise network transport, browser rules or server session handling.','That flag is directly read in the fixture.','That flag is directly read in the fixture.','The literal is explicitly printed.']
]},
{id:'web-build-version-deploy',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Follow a website from source changes to release',introduction:'A university corrects its registration dates. Editing a local file, recording a commit, building assets and deploying a release are different steps. Keeping them separate prevents the team from announcing a fix that has not reached users.',sections:[s('Source control records a change, not a release','A repository stores source and history. A commit records a snapshot with a message and parent history; it is not automatically copied to every web server. A branch supports an isolated line of work, and a review compares proposed changes before integration. Generated assets may be produced from source during a build.','A deployment places a particular application/artifact version into an environment. Configuration such as public hostnames and server-only credentials can differ across development, testing and production. Do not embed private keys into downloadable browser files or assume local settings are suitable for production.'),s('Make releases traceable and reversible','Record the source revision and build result associated with a release. Test the intended user behavior in a controlled environment, verify critical routes after release under approved checks and retain an appropriate rollback strategy. Cached assets may keep an old browser version alive, so compatible contracts and asset versioning matter.','The fixture compares release and commit identifiers in a synthetic manifest. It does not push code, build assets or contact a hosting platform. A rollback is not automatically safe for database changes; data compatibility requires deliberate planning beyond this introductory content.')],worked:w('manifest = {"source_commit":"demo-a7", "built_commit":"demo-a7", "deployed_commit":"demo-a6"}\nprint(manifest["source_commit"] == manifest["built_commit"])\nprint(manifest["built_commit"] == manifest["deployed_commit"])','True\nFalse','Identify whether a synthetic built revision has reached the deployed environment.',['Read the three recorded revisions.','Compare source and build identity.','Compare build and deployment identity.'],'The recorded values show a deployment gap in the fixture; they are not a live hosting-status check.'),mistakes:['Calling a local commit a production deployment.','Putting server secrets in client-delivered configuration.'],takeaways:['Track source, build and deployed revision separately.','Validate release behavior and compatibility rather than infer it from a commit message.'],quiz:[
['What does a Git commit primarily record?','A source-history snapshot','A guaranteed public release','Every user’s current browser cache','A database backup by definition','A commit records repository state and history; release and runtime state are separate.','Deployment needs a separate process.','Browser caches are not commit contents.','A source commit is not inherently a data backup.'],
['What is a build artifact?','Output prepared from source','The domain registrar’s invoice','Only an HTTP status code','Only a browser bookmark','Build systems can transform source into assets or packages intended for execution or deployment.','Registration billing is not build output.','A status is a response result.','Bookmarks store addresses.'],
['Where should private server credentials be kept?','Protected server configuration','A public browser bundle','A source comment on the landing page','An image filename','Credentials intended only for server use must not be delivered publicly to clients.','Downloaded bundles can be inspected.','Comments can disclose secrets.','Filenames are not secret storage.'],
['A release manifest says built a7, deployed a6. What does that indicate?','The new build is not deployed there','The a7 build necessarily failed','Every user sees a7 already','All database records were restored','The recorded deployed revision differs from the new built revision.','A successful build can remain undeployed.','The manifest says otherwise for that environment.','Revision mismatch does not prove data restoration.'],
['A local date correction is committed. What must be verified before announcing it live?','The intended release behavior','Only the commit message spelling','Only the editor theme','Only the laptop hostname','A repository change must reach and behave correctly in the intended environment.','Messages do not establish runtime behavior.','Themes do not deploy code.','A laptop name does not identify production release state.'],
['A rollback changes application code while schema changed incompatibly. What is the risk?','Code/data incompatibility','Automatic semantic repair','Guaranteed zero downtime','Guaranteed restoration of deleted rows','Rollback safety depends on compatibility with current data and schema.','Semantics are not repaired automatically.','A rollback can fail or cause outage.','Changing code does not restore deleted data.'],
['Some browsers still use old cached scripts after release. What should be considered?','Asset and API compatibility','Only the new logo size','Only the branch display name','Only browser bookmark order','Old clients can remain active, so versioning and compatible interfaces need review.','Logo size does not address API changes.','Branch labels do not update cached assets.','Bookmarks do not determine script compatibility.'],
['Does the synthetic revision-comparison code deploy anything?','No, it compares supplied data','Yes, it pushes all branches','Yes, it changes the host configuration','Yes, it restores a backup','The example has no network or deployment operation; it illustrates release traceability.','No Git push occurs.','No hosting API is called.','No backup operation is performed.']
]},
{id:'web-safe-professional-use',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Recognize browser trust and account-safety decisions',introduction:'A team member receives an unexpected message asking them to sign into a payroll portal through a new link. Basic web literacy combines the intended hostname, safe credential handling and an understanding that browser UI state does not replace server permission.',sections:[s('Read the destination before granting authority','A visually familiar page can exist at an unrelated hostname. Inspect the actual destination and use an independently trusted entry point when a request is unexpected. HTTPS protects a connection to the selected name; it does not make a lookalike name the organization’s intended service. Avoid sending passwords or codes through an unverified support message.','Browser extensions and pasted scripts may have significant authority. Grant only justified permissions and follow organizational installation policy. Downloading a file or granting a permission should follow a clear user intention; a decorative prompt is not sufficient assurance.'),s('Separate usability signals from authorization','A hidden admin link does not protect an admin API, and a disabled button does not prevent someone from sending a request. The server must enforce identity, permissions and input constraints for the actual operation. Report suspected unintended access through the approved channel using minimal evidence rather than exploring other people’s records.','The fixture checks an exact allowlisted hostname in synthetic URLs. It is not a phishing detector, certificate validator or complete URL security policy: ports, redirects, Unicode names, path purpose and organization policy need explicit handling. Its useful lesson is to compare a parsed host, not search for a familiar substring.')],worked:w('from urllib.parse import urlsplit\nallowed = {"portal.example.test"}\nfor url in ["https://portal.example.test/login", "https://portal.example.test.unrelated.test/login"]:\n    print(urlsplit(url).hostname in allowed)','True\nFalse','Compare exact hostnames rather than familiar-looking URL text.',['Parse each synthetic address.','Compare its hostname to the declared allowed set.','Reject a longer unrelated hostname despite its familiar prefix.'],'Only the exact hostname decision is modeled. This is not complete browser or organizational trust verification.'),mistakes:['Trusting a familiar name anywhere inside a URL.','Testing access by browsing other real users’ private records.'],takeaways:['Use trusted entry points and precise destination checks.','Report access-control concerns with minimal authorized evidence.'],quiz:[
['What is safer than searching for a familiar word in a URL?','Compare the parsed hostname','Trust the first visible substring','Trust every domain with a padlock','Ignore the destination after clicking','A familiar substring may appear inside an unrelated hostname; exact parsed identity matters.','Prefixes can be deceptive.','TLS alone does not identify the intended organization.','The destination is part of the trust decision.'],
['Does hiding an administrator link secure its API?','No, the server must authorize','Yes, hidden URLs cannot be requested','Yes, CSS creates server permissions','Only if the icon is removed too','Presentation restrictions cannot replace server enforcement of the requested operation.','Requests can be sent without the link.','CSS does not enforce server policy.','Icon removal is still presentation.'],
['How should suspected unintended data access be reported?','Use minimal approved evidence','Collect every private record','Publish raw credentials','Repeat changes on real accounts','Responsible reporting minimizes private data and stays within the approved scope.','Bulk collection increases harm and exceeds the need.','Credentials must remain protected.','Real account mutations are not justified.'],
['A link is https://portal.example.test.unrelated.test. Which hostname is present?','portal.example.test.unrelated.test','portal.example.test only','unrelated as a path','No hostname because HTTPS is used','The full parsed hostname includes every label before the path.','A prefix is not the complete host.','There is no path separator before unrelated.','HTTPS URLs still have hosts.'],
['A page asks for a password after an unexpected message. What is a prudent first step?','Use a trusted independent entry point','Send the password to confirm identity','Disable certificate checks','Install any requested extension','An independently known destination reduces reliance on the unexpected message’s claimed authority.','Sending a secret does not verify the recipient.','Disabling checks removes protection.','Extensions can add substantial authority.'],
['A disabled payment button is the only operation guard. What is missing?','Server-side enforcement','A second disabled icon','A longer button label','A different font family','A client control can be bypassed; the server needs actual identity, permission and state checks.','More presentation controls do not enforce the API.','Labels do not constrain direct requests.','Fonts do not define authorization.'],
['An extension requests broad page access. What should guide approval?','Permission policy','Only its colourful logo','Only its download count','Only its short description','Permissions should be justified and reviewed under the applicable trust policy.','Branding does not establish authority safety.','Popularity does not prove suitability.','A description alone does not validate behavior.'],
['An exact-host fixture returns True. What must not be inferred?','The entire site is trustworthy','The supplied hostname matched','The allowlist contained that host','The parser extracted a host','One comparison does not validate certificates, redirects, business intent or application security.','That is the modeled condition.','Membership is what the fixture tests.','Parsing is explicitly executed.']
]}
];
